Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "block_timestamp": extract the calendar year / month and the exact number of seconds in that month ( epoch is stored in micro‑seconds).  
• "transaction_hash": count every transaction record (duplicates intentionally kept).  
• "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP": same as "block_timestamp" but in the GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.DECODED_EVENTS table (could be used for cross‑checking).  

Typical filter conditions that may be helpful  
• WHERE DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp"/1e6)) = 2023  
• WHERE "block_timestamp" BETWEEN <first‑day‑of‑2023‑month‑seconds × 1e6> AND <first‑day‑of‑next‑month‑seconds × 1e6>  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20) that go from simple inspection to the final monthly‑aggregation pattern.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotes as required.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Inspect a handful of raw log rows to confirm column presence and value formats.
SELECT
  "transaction_hash",
  "block_timestamp",
  "block_number",
  "address"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Peek at the minimum and maximum timestamps to verify the overall time span covered.
SELECT
  MIN("block_timestamp") AS "min_ts",
  MAX("block_timestamp") AS "max_ts"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: List distinct calendar years present in the dataset (micro‑second epoch → seconds → timestamp).
SELECT DISTINCT
  DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) AS "year"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Show all year‑month combinations seen during 2023 (helps verify completeness).
SELECT DISTINCT
  DATE_PART('year',  TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))  AS "year",
  DATE_PART('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))  AS "month"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
WHERE
  DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
ORDER BY
  "month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Count ALL log rows for the whole year 2023 (duplicates of the same hash included).
SELECT
  COUNT(*) AS "total_log_rows_2023"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
WHERE
  DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Show how many raw log rows exist per month in 2023.
SELECT
  DATE_PART('year',  TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))  AS "year",
  DATE_PART('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))  AS "month",
  COUNT(*)                                             AS "monthly_log_rows"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
WHERE
  DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
GROUP BY
  "year", "month"
ORDER BY
  "monthly_log_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Derive the first day‑of‑month timestamp and the next month’s first day to help compute seconds in month.
SELECT
  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))                           AS "month_start",
  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) + INTERVAL '1 month'      AS "next_month_start"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
WHERE
  DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Verify that different tables yield comparable row counts (LOGS vs DECODED_EVENTS) for January 2023.
SELECT
  'LOGS'                                         AS "table",
  COUNT(*)                                       AS "row_cnt"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
WHERE
  TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) BETWEEN '2023-01-01' AND '2023-01-31'
UNION ALL
SELECT
  'DECODED_EVENTS'                               AS "table",
  COUNT(*)                                       AS "row_cnt"
FROM
  GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.DECODED_EVENTS
WHERE
  TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP("BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" / 1e6)) BETWEEN '2023-01-01' AND '2023-01-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Compute the exact seconds in each month of 2023 (helps with TPS calculation).
SELECT
  "year_month",
  DATEDIFF('second', "month_start", "next_month_start") AS "seconds_in_month"
FROM (
  SELECT DISTINCT
    DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))                       AS "month_start",
    DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) + INTERVAL '1 month'  AS "next_month_start",
    TO_CHAR(DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)), 'YYYY-MM')   AS "year_month"
  FROM
    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
  WHERE
    DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
) t
ORDER BY
  "year_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final pattern – monthly transaction (log row) count plus computed transactions‑per‑second (TPS) for 2023.
SELECT
  "year",
  "month",
  "monthly_txn_cnt",
  ROUND("monthly_txn_cnt" / "seconds_in_month", 6) AS "txns_per_second"
FROM (
  SELECT
    DATE_PART('year',  TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))               AS "year",
    DATE_PART('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6))               AS "month",
    COUNT(*)                                                                AS "monthly_txn_cnt",
    DATEDIFF(
      'second',
      DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)),
      DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) + INTERVAL '1 month'
    )                                                                       AS "seconds_in_month"
  FROM
    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
  WHERE
    DATE_PART('year', TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1e6)) = 2023
  GROUP BY
    "year",
    "month"
) t
ORDER BY
  "monthly_txn_cnt" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progress from simple data inspection to the final aggregation that produces the monthly transaction counts and their corresponding transactions‑per‑second values for 2023.
